{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Cojocaru2004-QW-periodic-N50-npa035",
  "Source": "E. Cojocaru, \"Quarter-wave discrete wavelet design of dichroic highly reflecting-transmitting mirrors for ultrafast solid-state lasers,\" Appl. Opt. 43, 1261-1265 (2004). doi:10.1364/AO.43.001261 - Secs. 3-4, p. 1262 and Fig. 3 (dashed): N = 50 alternating QW layers n1 = na + npa = 2.25, n2 = na - npa = 1.55, n0 = ns = na = 1.9, lambda0 = 1 um",
  "Notes": "Periodic quarter-wave reference stack compared with the QWDW in Fig. 3\nSpecification: Periodic QW reflector with the same N, na and npa as the QWDW.\nReported: Fig. 3(a): full width at R = 0.5 almost equal to the QWDW's, Eq. (4): dl/l0 ~ (4/pi)(0.35/1.9) = 0.2345; strong sidelobes. Fig. 3(b): R0 at lambda0 higher than the QWDW's (dashed curve reaches ~1). Analytic R0 = tanh^2[(N/2) ln(n1/n2)] = 0.99999997.\nNotes: Whether H or L is first is not stated; with n0 = ns the reflectance is the same for either order. Normal incidence; incident medium and substrate both n = na = 1.9 (n0 = ns = na), non-dispersive and lossless, so R is the same whichever side faces the light.\nLayers from Secs. 3-4, p. 1262 and Fig. 3 (dashed): N = 50 alternating QW layers n1 = na + npa = 2.25, n2 = na - npa = 1.55, n0 = ns = na = 1.9, lambda0 = 1 um; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours R0 0.99999997 at 1000 nm; paper above the wavelet stack (Fig. 3b).",
  "Wavelength": [
    1000
  ],
